    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nIn mappings that create cursors, move and start insert mode, the insert edits are replayed for all cursors, but the insert edit is replayed at all cursor positions.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. nvim --clean\n2. :xmap I Q0i\n3. create some lines of text:\n```\ntest\nnvim\n\ntest\nnvim\n```\n4. press `gg$Qjjjq=VjI`\n5. type `bad`, exit insert mode\n6. result:\n```\ntesbadt\nnvim\n\nbadtesbadt\nnvibadm\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nDepending on how exactly replays of mappings that create cursors and do something else is supposed to be handled one of the following:\n1. As it would currently happen if you typed out the map on your own:\n```\nbadtest\nnvim\n\nbadtest\nbadnvim\n```\n2. What would theortically happen if the mapping is replayed for each cursor that exists before the start and the substeps are replayed to all cursors that exist during their execution(also what I wanted to achieve, but might not be a good default/wanted):\n```\nbadtest\nbadnvim\n\nbadtest\nbadnvim\n```\n3. As what happens with non-insert mode edits, e.g. with `:xmap I Q0P` with `bad` in register\n```\ntest\nnvim\n\nbadtest\nnvim\n```\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-nightly+2124f96\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo, doesn't have this functionality\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS Linux x86_64 7.2.2\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.2-dev+e8aa098-nix\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nnix-community/neovim-nightly-overlay  with updated neovim source",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
